  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "If an arc of \\(45^\\circ\\) on circle \\(A\\) has the same length as an arc of \\(30^\\circ\\) on circle \\(B,\\) then the ratio of the area of circle \\(A\\) to the area of circle \\(B\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{9}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{6}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "An arc occupies the same fraction of a circle's circumference as its degree measure occupies of \\(360^\\circ\\). Let \\(r_A>0\\) and \\(r_B>0\\) be the radii of the two circles. Equal arc lengths give\n\\[\n\\frac{45}{360}\\cdot2\\pi r_A\n=\n\\frac{30}{360}\\cdot2\\pi r_B.\n\\]\nCanceling the common factor gives \\(45r_A=30r_B\\), so\n\\[\n\\frac{r_A}{r_B}=\\frac23.\n\\]\nBecause a circle's area is \\(\\pi r^2\\), the requested ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi r_A^2}{\\pi r_B^2}\n=\\left(\\frac23\\right)^2\n=\\boxed{\\frac49},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
